#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504717} :activity/id #uuid "fb6cf230-95ba-52e0-b584-587b6c68c3e0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048555 :topic/id #uuid "a95c2c28-8d05-5d82-baa9-f51e879c16c9" :topic/math-academy-id 3127} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347c0583-8380-5437-bea6-2f27bbcf1b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504120} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fd3eca-fb7c-56fe-9145-27b9896b8a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504350} {:answer/id #uuid "e76e528c-2465-542a-9649-85a4f3aa9713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504432} {:answer/id #uuid "ea32c5e0-4b1f-5fce-827c-bcd543830e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504438} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb074c9-89ae-5600-83ef-9cf2870ac238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504432} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1da4cf-719f-5bbd-9c32-cdb26adbbe05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8c9dba6-d928-5ac1-ba3e-1db8d33cfb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-94793" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -45 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "10c29fe9-08fc-5279-949b-8249dea25825" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abae4a38-c5fd-5f02-a772-5aa129094bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469734} {:answer/id #uuid "61d28482-9aa5-5496-9fe2-e88afd3b2c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469734} :answer-field/id #uuid "1888ce5d-ffbd-5ed4-8be1-6bd4f78e3048" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c36a82ef-f663-5517-a82d-dfb31295d4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469736} {:answer/id #uuid "1abaa9d3-1384-5eab-9d1f-4167841b8420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469737}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469736} :answer-field/id #uuid "78bab090-2cec-52a7-8c1a-ea25848b4605" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470480} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8053ba89-f2f1-5607-8419-7cfe23f95b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469738} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6d6721-833a-5366-b67a-9b57255478fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469739} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fa5bdc-7dac-5793-b27a-1eadf77bbfa1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a997acb-de91-5ebd-a6cc-103226757603" :question/math-academy-id "q-94787" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* positive-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive (${\\lambda }_{1}=-6$ is not positive).\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "380d136a-e07a-515f-bfdc-c12a82904a6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c25508-a03c-54ed-af75-528b36334d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464054} {:answer/id #uuid "f997d662-f0fe-5970-be50-6cced7ae7b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464055} :answer-field/id #uuid "f09da3cc-6896-57c1-b85f-50c6209f8fae" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30af1cd-d4ae-5f09-b616-cad8cd88ec73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} {:answer/id #uuid "6256dff6-b788-5cb5-8d29-52c667534b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464056} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c4ab03-9594-5b6b-ae8b-d04de998a6a6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50467cc1-00c3-5d22-812d-d54f3f3960c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0b1cda-a69f-5c84-8c20-b1e4a0fb21d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464058} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3999868-4945-5e38-b4fa-18fc7ca1a6ff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30844de8-e636-5e8f-ba19-823c3f8b1cf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139455" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n- **I.** ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n- **III.** ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative semi-definite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $S$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ is positive).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3b3b3471-3890-502c-9e74-ac7e9eadbeba"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420ec396-a215-5051-bcb8-10ed9acc95ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452101} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c27e94-c52e-5c96-8d89-053964a8a2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452101} :answer-field/id #uuid "83818ab7-8e39-5903-9ee3-959ef2554040" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c441b03-7a1e-59f5-9515-8053ce5355d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452103} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8eaa01-f5f3-50e0-9b13-165b38fc8277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452104} :answer-field/id #uuid "82bbe034-c71c-5eb5-aefd-fd09fd107a27" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897837ee-7319-5a6c-a781-a2bf0d97f197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452105} {:answer/id #uuid "317ed741-0f0a-53c2-9948-7ac37ceef348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452106} :answer-field/id #uuid "536fbf80-08b5-5e1a-a2ac-b0eae9c1cf2c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "43115d1d-8694-5a95-b413-4600fbd2bc68" :question/math-academy-id "q-94804" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-5$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-5$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3edbcb74-ef0e-5d2b-b4a5-9224616cec9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e082f9d-d667-53bf-8ebe-ea0165b6dc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452107} {:answer/id #uuid "1b961c92-afa7-5358-90fd-24c675c9bd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452107} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d091f6-4c30-55e8-95e8-5336bea05763" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e233fee2-70e2-5e90-896f-04fbc826023e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452109} {:answer/id #uuid "a851a8d9-1e67-5532-b8dd-92c9d9b65acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452110} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be5e419-a12d-5643-8194-2d9a2b4897ec" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0fe63a9-b90e-5777-b826-3d8827fd649a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452111} {:answer/id #uuid "1aded71f-8051-53a7-97fa-e199f9608ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452111} :answer-field/id #uuid "42458164-cc6a-5528-9e47-6288386413d8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d5f731a-8eca-5400-88d7-dd10fc541120" :question/math-academy-id "q-94805" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=8$ and $det(A)<0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=8$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=8>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=21>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c73b7c66-857e-5a2c-9637-ba3dda90e9f0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504718}}] :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504030}]}
